From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 07:27:46 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 071/119] scene-referred S2: the skybox times-ten, an
 instrument to prove it, and the Nishita slot

Third item of Stage S2. The skybox is the only light source in the engine whose authored unit and
delivered unit differ by an order of magnitude, and the procedural sky is on a third scale that
cannot be derived from source at all. This gates the first and builds the measuring instrument
both of them need.

Decision: scene-notes/decisions/s2-sky-scale.md, approach A. Research:
scene-notes/research/s2-skybox-emittance-collision.md.

- Renderer.cpp's "translate to kilo-scale" times-ten becomes a skyEmittanceScale local, 1.0 on
  the scene-referred path. The comment there describes a convention (1.0 = 1000 cd/m^2) that
  exists nowhere else in the engine and that nothing downstream undoes: Sky.cpp uploads the value
  raw and Stars.cfx multiplies the sky texture by it straight into the engine-unit HDR target. A
  textured skybox therefore delivers ten times its authored lux, +3.32 stops, which with the
  shipped defaults makes the sky out-shine the sun and is the most likely single cause of CE's
  flat, hazy default daylight. It is invisible in a stock project only because the shipped
  sky.mtl carries no diffuse texture, so the branch is never taken - the trap springs the moment
  someone authors a real sky.

  The material emittance added below it is deliberately untouched: GetFinalEmittance() converts
  kcd/m^2 to engine units correctly and shares that factor with particles, fog and HUD3D. The
  scale is a local applied to the first addend only, so that under the gate the two addends
  finally agree and off the gate they keep their existing 100x disagreement byte for byte.

- The decision note requires this to be PROVEN before it is changed, and the measurement needs a
  running engine, which this session cannot start. So the change ships with its own instrument:
  r_HDRDebug 1 gains a fourth line,

      Skybox: authored N lux -> renderer N cd/m2 (implies N lux, ratio N.NNx)

  where "authored" is recovered exactly from E3DPARAM_SKY_SKYBOX_EMITTANCE (the inverse of
  ConvertIlluminanceToLightColor is exact), "renderer" is what UpdateSkyInfo actually left in
  m_SkyInfo, and "implies" is E = pi * L, the illuminance a uniform sky of that luminance
  delivers on a horizontal surface. With correct units the ratio reads 1.00x. Flipping the
  camera's Scene Referred property with that line on screen is the whole proof: 10.0x becomes
  1.00x. The line reports when no sky texture is assigned, because in that state the term is
  computed and then not used.

- The Nishita sky gets the calibration slot, not a calibration. m_sunIntensity is scaled by
  r_SceneReferredNishitaScale (VF_NULL, default 1.0, so uncalibrated is exactly stock) in the
  same shape as the neighbouring 1e-4f rescale of Km and Kr. It is a branch and not an
  unconditional multiply by 1.0f so the stock path compiles and evaluates the original
  expression. The engine authors' own comment on that line ("for more convenient editing in time
  of day dialog") says these numbers were tuned by eye; RENDERER_LIGHT_UNIT_SCALE appears in none
  of the SkyLight files. Deriving the factor from source is not possible, so the honest move is
  to measure it.

- docs/SceneReferredCalibration.md documents all three measurements that S2 leaves open: the
  times-ten proof, the Nishita procedure from research section 6 (18 % ground plane, sun-only /
  skybox-only / dome-only steps, target ~12 000 lux diffuse at 45 degrees elevation, plus the
  20/70 degree verification whose residual is recorded rather than fudged), and the sun-shaft
  strength bound from item 4. It also says where results go: into the .env preset and the cvar,
  never into default.env and never into EnvironmentPreset.cpp, whose defaults and ranges are
  shared with the stock path.

Not fixed, deliberately: the dead store that stops the Nishita sun fading at night
(TimeOfDay.cpp:1168). Correcting it would change the stock path's night sky. Documented instead.

Built clean (0 errors, 0 warnings outside Code/SDKs): Cry3DEngine, CryRenderD3D11,
CinematicCamera.

