@@ -0,0 +1,143 @@
+# Scene-Referred Calibration Procedures
+
+Measurements that cannot be derived from the engine's source and have to be made once, in a
+running engine, and recorded. Everything here belongs to Stage S2 of `SceneReferredSpec.md`.
+
+Nothing in this document is a code change. Each procedure ends in a number you write into a cvar
+or into a `.env` preset.
+
+---
+
+## 0. The instrument
+
+`r_HDRDebug 1` draws four lines in the top-left corner:
+
+```
+Average Luminance (cd/m2): ...
+Estimated Illuminance (lux): ...
+Scene-referred EV: ...  (metered EV: ...)  pre-exposure x...
+Skybox: authored N lux -> renderer N cd/m2 (implies N lux, ratio N.NNx)
+```
+
+* **Estimated Illuminance** is a whole-frame average read back from the engine's luminance-measure
+  pass. It is a frame average, not a per-direction measurement, so a test scene must fill the
+  frame with the thing being measured.
+* **Skybox** is the S2 unit readout. `authored` is the "Skybox / Intensity (lux)" you typed in the
+  time-of-day panel, recovered exactly. `implies` is the illuminance a uniform sky of the
+  luminance the renderer is actually using would deliver on a horizontal surface, `E = pi * L`.
+  With correct units the two are equal and `ratio` reads `1.00x`.
+* The last line appends `[no sky texture: term unused]` when the sky material has no diffuse
+  texture. In that state the skybox path is not drawn at all and the numbers are informational
+  only — this is exactly why the times-ten went unnoticed in stock CRYENGINE.
+
+---
+
+## 1. The skybox times-ten (proof, one look)
+
+**What is being tested.** `S3DEngineCommon::UpdateSkyInfo` multiplied the authored skybox
+emittance by ten ("translate to kilo-scale") into a convention that exists nowhere else in the
+engine and is never undone. The scene-referred path gates that multiply to 1.0. This confirms it.
+
+**Setup.** Any level with a sky material that has an HDR diffuse texture assigned (RC preset
+`SkyboxHDR` / `HDRSkydomeRGBK_highQ`, BC6UH). Set `Skybox / Opacity` to 1 so the skybox path is
+selected and the procedural dome is off.
+
+**Procedure.**
+
+1. `r_HDRDebug 1`.
+2. With the Cinematic Camera's **Scene Referred** property OFF, read the `Skybox:` line.
+   Expect `ratio` ≈ **10.0x**.
+3. Turn **Scene Referred** ON. Read it again. Expect `ratio` ≈ **1.00x** and `renderer cd/m2`
+   ten times smaller.
+
+**Result to record.** The two ratios. If step 2 does not read ~10x, stop — the times-ten is
+compensating something we have not found, and the fix belongs where the compensation is
+(`decisions/s2-sky-scale.md` approach B, the unread `SkyDome_SkyBoxParams.z` lever, is the
+fallback).
+
+**Note on the divide by PI.** The skybox keeps its `÷pi` on both paths. It is not the BRDF
+compensation the sun's was — the sky never enters a BRDF — it is the illuminance-to-luminance
+conversion of a uniform hemisphere, and it is what makes "Intensity (lux)" mean the number a
+light meter pointed at the ground reads. Only the times-ten was wrong.
+
+---
+
+## 2. The Nishita sky scale (`r_SceneReferredNishitaScale`)
+
+**Why it needs measuring.** The procedural sky is a third brightness system. `m_sunIntensity` has
+no documented unit and `RENDERER_LIGHT_UNIT_SCALE` appears in none of the `SkyLight*` files, so
+its relationship to the photometric skybox path cannot be read out of the source. The engine
+authors' own comment on the neighbouring line ("scale mie and rayleigh scattering for more
+convenient editing in the time of day dialog") says plainly that these numbers were tuned by eye.
+
+**Test level.**
+
+1. A flat ground plane of albedo exactly **0.18** filling the lower hemisphere. (18 % is the ACES
+   anchor; see spec D6.)
+2. No fog, no probes, no local lights, no SSAO, no SVOGI.
+3. Camera at eye height looking at the ground so the frame is dominated by the patch.
+4. Cinematic Camera **Scene Referred** ON, exposure Manual and locked at a known EV100.
+
+**Procedure.**
+
+| Step | Configuration | Read | Expect |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| A | Sun only: `Skybox / Opacity = 0`, `Sky Light / Sun intensity multiplier = 0` | Estimated Illuminance | `Sun intensity (lux) x cos(elevation)` within a few percent |
+| B | Skybox only, uniform white 1.0 texture, sun 0 | Estimated Illuminance | `Skybox intensity (lux)`. This is section 1's proof again, from the other side. |
+| C | Nishita only: skybox opacity 0, sun intensity 0, sun elevation 45 deg | Estimated Illuminance | some value `E_nishita` |
+| D | — | — | set `r_SceneReferredNishitaScale = E_target / E_nishita` |
+
+**Choosing `E_target`.** The Nishita dome is a clear-sky atmosphere model, so the honest target is
+the real clear-sky diffuse illuminance at the same sun elevation: with the sun at 45 degrees,
+**≈ 12 000 lux** on a horizontal surface (direct normal ≈ 90 000 lux, i.e. ≈ 64 000 lux horizontal,
+is the *sun*, measured in step A — do not add it here).
+
+**Verification.** Repeat step C at 20 and 70 degrees elevation with the factor applied. It will not
+match exactly at all three; the model has its own angular response. Record the residual as the
+honest error of the model rather than fudging the constant. If the residual exceeds about a stop,
+the right answer is a time-of-day curve authored by the user, not a single constant.
+
+**Acceptance (spec S2).** Step B reads the authored lux within 5 %. Step C reads within 0.25 stops
+of the target at 45 degrees. A daylight ReC preset shows a measured sun/shade ratio of at least
+10 stops — measurable as `E_sunlit / E_shadow` on two patches of the same 18 % material.
+
+**Known limitation, not a bug to fix here.** The Nishita sun intensity does not fade at night: the
+dawn/dusk fade computed in `CTimeOfDay::UpdateEnvLighting` is unconditionally overwritten a few
+lines later. Fixing it would change the stock path's night sky, so it is left alone. If the ReC
+preset uses the skybox path the issue does not arise.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Sun shaft strength (`Sun Shaft Amount`)
+
+**Why it needs measuring.** On the stock path the shaft colour is normalised to unit length, so
+shaft brightness is `SunShafts_RaysAmount` and nothing else, and the working targets are UNORM so
+the mask is a clipped silhouette. On the scene-referred path the colour is the sun's actual
+exposed radiance and the mask is a real luminance image, so the same 0.25 is several stops
+brighter and the parameter has become a scattering coefficient.
+
+**Physical bound.** A shaft is single-scattered sunlight through participating media. Its radiance
+is bounded by roughly `L_sun * sigma_s * pathlength`; for clear air that is a small fraction of the
+sky's radiance and far below the solar disc. A defensible ceiling: **shafts should never exceed
+the sky's radiance in the same direction, and typically sit 2 to 4 stops below it.**
+
+**Procedure.** In the section 2 test level with the sky at a known luminance, raise the camera's
+own shaft amount until the shafts read at the sky's luminance, then back off 2 to 4 stops. Record
+the value. Expect the physical setting to be of order **0.01 to 0.05**, not 0.25.
+
+**Check under motion.** The composite now happens before the tone map, which puts an unclipped,
+high-contrast, camera-motion-dependent radial pattern in front of the temporal resolve. Pan the
+camera across the sun and watch for highlight crawl before signing the value off.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Recording results
+
+Write measured values into:
+
+* `r_SceneReferredNishitaScale` — into the project's `.cryproject` or the ReC preset's
+  documentation, not into code.
+* Sky and sun lux — into the `.env` preset (`ReC_Physical.env`), never into `default.env` and
+  never into `EnvironmentPreset.cpp`, whose defaults and ranges are shared with the stock path.
+* The residuals and the date — into `scene-notes/research/s2-*.md`, so the next session does not
